From 8ab5cfa54bc79e8490387269ed50759e80660b00 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sridharan S N <sridsn@qti.qualcomm.com>
Date: Wed, 22 Jul 2026 08:05:42 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] rpmsg: glink: validate OPEN channel name NUL-termination in
 rx_defer

A remote processor sending a GLINK_CMD_OPEN command can supply a channel
name that contains no NUL terminator within the declared length.  When
qcom_glink_rx_defer() allocates and fills the glink_defer_cmd buffer the
allocation is exactly sized to the declared length (after 8-byte
alignment), so kzalloc() zeroes only the slack bytes that exist for
non-exact-fit slab sizes.  For exact-fit sizes (extra = 40, 104, 232 or
488 on arm64, producing 64-, 128-, 256- and 512-byte kmalloc objects)
there are no slack bytes and the name bytes fill the object completely.

The deferred worker then passes msg->data to qcom_glink_rx_open() as a
plain C string.  strcmp() and kstrdup() both call strlen() on that
pointer, walking past the end of the dcmd allocation into the adjacent
slab object until a NUL byte is found.  This constitutes:

  - a kernel heap out-of-bounds read (information disclosure: adjacent
    slab object bytes are copied into channel->name by kstrdup() and
    may be exposed through driver log paths), and
  - a potential kernel fault if the walk crosses an unmapped page
    boundary (denial of service / device reload).

Fix: in qcom_glink_rx_defer(), after copying the raw FIFO bytes into
dcmd but before queuing it, validate that:
  1. extra > 0 (name cannot be zero-length),
  2. extra <= GLINK_NAME_SIZE (matches the transmit-side cap in
     qcom_glink_send_open_req()), and
  3. memchr(dcmd->msg.data, NUL, extra) finds a NUL terminator within
     the declared length.

If validation fails, free dcmd, advance the RX FIFO (so the interrupt
handler does not re-process the same malformed message on the next
interrupt), log an error, and return -EINVAL.

This is the earliest point at which the declared length (extra) is
still available for a precise memchr check; by the time the deferred
worker calls qcom_glink_rx_open() only the raw char * pointer remains.

Change-Id: Ide1489dfaa671dab3e52e88c61cb90d647bd6b16
Signed-off-by: Sridharan S N <sridsn@qti.qualcomm.com>
---
 drivers/rpmsg/qcom_glink_native.c | 23 +++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 23 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/rpmsg/qcom_glink_native.c b/drivers/rpmsg/qcom_glink_native.c
index 5f748c09226d..e4c23a256408 100644
--- a/drivers/rpmsg/qcom_glink_native.c
+++ b/drivers/rpmsg/qcom_glink_native.c
@@ -985,6 +985,29 @@ static int qcom_glink_rx_defer(struct qcom_glink *glink, size_t extra)
 
 	qcom_glink_rx_peek(glink, &dcmd->msg, 0, sizeof(dcmd->msg) + extra);
 
+	/*
+	 * For OPEN commands, the remote-supplied channel name must be
+	 * NUL-terminated within the declared length and must not exceed
+	 * GLINK_NAME_SIZE (matching the transmit-side check in
+	 * qcom_glink_send_open_req()).
+	 * Without this check, strcmp() and kstrdup() in qcom_glink_rx_open()
+	 * walk past the end of the dcmd allocation into the adjacent slab
+	 * object (heap disclosure / kernel fault on unmapped boundary).
+	 * Advance the FIFO before returning so the interrupt handler does
+	 * not re-process the same malformed message.
+	 */
+	if (le16_to_cpu(dcmd->msg.cmd) == GLINK_CMD_OPEN) {
+		if (extra == 0 || extra > GLINK_NAME_SIZE ||
+		    !memchr(dcmd->msg.data, '\0', extra)) {
+			dev_err(glink->dev,
+				"OPEN channel name invalid (len=%zu)\n", extra);
+			kfree(dcmd);
+			qcom_glink_rx_advance(glink,
+					      ALIGN(sizeof(struct glink_msg) + extra, 8));
+			return -EINVAL;
+		}
+	}
+
 	rx_defer.cmd = le16_to_cpu(dcmd->msg.cmd);
 	rx_defer.ktime = ktime_to_ms(ktime_get());
 	rx_defer.param1 = le16_to_cpu(dcmd->msg.param1);
-- 
